st  likely to get dangerous levels of uv  exposure is also the time where you  don't feel like wearing as many clothes  finding uv protective clothing is also  really tricky which is why i'm going to  break it down in this video  upf is ultraviolet protection factor and  it's the most reliable way to tell how  much uv protection you're getting from  clothes  upf for clothing is just like spf for  sunscreen if you're wearing a piece of  upf 50 clothing that means only 1 50th  of the sunburn causing  or erythema uv is going to reach your  skin just like with spf the higher the  upf number the more uv protection you're  getting  the difference is with spf and sunscreen  that only happens if you apply two  milligrams per square centimeter so if  you apply the same amount that they use  in spf testing  most people don't apply this much and  it's really hard to get this much evenly  all over your skin  with upf what you see on the label is  what you get when you put on your  clothing  although this can change over the  lifetime of your clothing which we'll  talk a bit about later the upf rating  for a piece of clothing is determined by  a standardized test  scientists measure how much uv passes  through the fabric  this is called transmittance and this is  done without using human skin so it's an  in vitro test  erythema or sunburn causing uv is a  mixture of uvb  and a little bit of uva light so what  scientists do is they measure the uva  and uvb  transmittance separately and then they  use maths to work out how much will get  through the clothing to burn your skin  in general you actually get higher  protection from your upf clothing than  what the upf rating is  that's because upf is sort of like a  worst case scenario where  all of the uv is coming straight on into  your clothing through the pores  in real life clothing is 3d so if the  light is coming in this way  only this bit is going to get that  straight on um  uv whereas on your other bits it doesn't  go straight through and so you get a lot  more protection  obviously the upf rating is just for the  skin covered so if you're wearing upf  clothing and your fingers are exposed  you're not going to get any protection  on your fingers  there are also regulations around what  sorts of clothing can get upf ratings  so generally it has to cover enough skin  so for example  singlets and bikinis generally aren't  allowed to have upf ratings  some places also have a limit on the  highest upf you can claim just like with  sunscreen in australia the highest upf  you can claim  is 50 plus just like with spf  [Music]  so with upf clothing you know what  protection you can expect  not many clothes actually have upf  ratings but regular clothing can also  protect your skin from uv  it just isn't measured and we know this  because  of course most of us have gotten tan  lines at some point from clothes  unfortunately a lot of fabrics don't  actually provide that much uv protection  so in one study they found that  one-third of the clothes they looked at  one-third of the summer clothes  were actually less than upf-15 less than  half of the clothes they looked at had  greater than upf 30.  it can be really hard to compare  different fabrics because there are so  many variables that will change how much  uv will go through  that's why upf rated clothing is so good  in my opinion  because you know for sure how much uv  protection you're getting  so i'm going to talk about some of the  different variables that you need to  look at  and generally what you want to do is  just tick off as many boxes as you can  the type of material that the fabric's  made from makes a huge difference  natural fibers in general just aren't  very uv absorbent so i'm talking about  things like cotton  rayon viscose and linen in one study all  of the white cotton rayon and linen that  they tested were below upf5  even if you have really tightly woven  heavy natural fabrics a lot of the time  the upf that you get  is less than 20. wool nylon and silk are  better  but the best one is polyester polyester  is really good at absorbing uvb  because of its structure it's got  benzene rings which is similar to some  sunscreens  when comparing similar fabrics made of  polyester and cotton so similar weave  similar thickness  they found that in general polyester had  three to four times the upf  of the cotton fabrics the big problem is  that polyester is awful to wear in  summer  so it gets very sweaty it's not  breathable it's just a bit gross  but you can get blends of polyester with  other materials and so this becomes a  lot more comfortable and more breathable  the uv protection does get a bit diluted  but it's just a lot more practical to  wear  so this happens with things like newer  generation sports clothes  with newer generation sports materials  you can get lots of little layers of  fabric  in the same fabric and so you get a lot  higher upf  so for example some professional soccer  shirts are greater than upf 100  fabric isn't solid and so uv gets  through little holes little pores in the  fabric  so if you have a fabric with smaller  holes or less holes you're going to get  a higher uv  protection regardless of what material  you're using so there are a whole bunch  of factors that affect how big and how  many holes you get  firstly there's the thickness of the  fibers with thicker fibers you end up  with  better uv protection there's also the  way that the yarn is spun  with more twisted yarn you end up with a  more compact fabric so again  higher uv protection then there's also  the construction of the fabric so  how that thread is turned into a fabric  knitter fabrics which have a little bit  of stretch to them  tend to be better than woven fabrics  with no stretch so these are used for  things like business shirts  then there's also washing some fabrics  shrink a bit in the wash  and the whole shrink as well this  happens a lot with cotton but it's  really variable  so 