  the thickness of the fabric which way  you're stretching it but in general  you actually lose a lot of uv protection  with not very much stretch  so for example if you stretch a cotton  lycra blend by 15  you actually lose half of the upf  protection if you stretch your stocking  by thirty percent  the upf actually goes to one ninth and  this isn't very much so this is what it  looks like so generally for sun  protection  go for looser fitting clothing  especially if it's a stretchy material  with wet clothing you also tend to get  lower upf protection which you might  have already guessed if you've seen  a patch of clothing go see-through when  it's wet the problem is the uv  protection can also go  up when clothing's wet and it's pretty  much impossible to predict which way it  goes  in general cotton clothing tends to  decrease in uv protection when it's wet  so a dry t-shirt will be upf five to  nine  a wet t-shirt will be upf three to four  point five  but outside of that you pretty much  can't say either way  so if you're looking for uv protective  clothing what you want is  synthetic fabrics tightly woven fabrics  with tiny holes  you also want dark intense colors and  you want them to cover as much of your  body as possible  unfortunately what this also means is  that it's probably not going to be very  nice to wear if you're out in the sun  and very active so i think that's the  big advantage of upf rated clothing  you can get things like special  technologies within the fibers or added  to the fibers  and so you can find comfortable clothes  that will give you a guaranteed level of  uv protection  that means you can also cut down how  long it takes to reapply sunscreen  and you don't have to worry about  getting the sunscreen on hard to reach  places  [Music]  so here are some brands of upf clothing  i've actually tried  this is a hoodie from uniqlo's uv range  it's got really high uv protection so  upf 50 even though it's really light and  cool so they 